  [PASS] A1 THE PREDICTION IS NON-MONOTONIC, which the low-z expansion in item 79 could not see: the published fit makes a_0 RISE to a maximum and then FALL back through its present value.  That shape, not the local slope, is the thing a survey at two redshifts can test   (maximum a_0(z)/a_0(0) = 1.0615 (+0.0259 dex) at z = 0.41; back through 1 at z = 1.06)
  [PASS] A2 and over the window where a_0 has actually been measured the CPL curve is already past its maximum and DECLINING -- the same direction the measurement leans.  So the published dark-energy fit and the framework's flat law are not opposites there; they are two nearby lines, and the data cannot be expected to tell them apart   (d log a_0/dz over z = 0.6-2.5 is -0.0642 for the CPL fit against 0.0000 for the framework -- a separation of only 0.0642 dex per unit z, and of the SAME sign as the measurement in Part B)

======================================================================================================================
PART B -- the closed-form inversion, reproduced, then differentiated with respect to the stellar mass-to-light ratio
======================================================================================================================
  99 of 100 galaxies invert; median a_0 = 1.385e-10 m/s^2 (+0.170 dex from canonical, +0.088 from alt)
     d log a_0/dz = -0.1123 +- 0.0642
  [PASS] B1 item 16 is reproduced here from the same table, which is the precondition for differentiating it   (this script -0.1123 +- 0.0642 against item 16's -0.112 +- 0.063)
  
  the lever: scaling the assumed stellar mass-to-light ratio by a factor U scales the STELLAR part of the
  baryonic mass inside R_e, hence 1 - f_DM.  RC100 discs at z = 1-2 are gas-rich, so the stellar share of the
  baryons is bracketed at 0.5 and 0.8 rather than assumed.
    f_star      U   median a_0   dex shift   d log a0/dlog U   d log a0/dz
       0.5   0.80    2.168e-10       0.195                         -0.0806
       0.5   1.25    1.335e-10      -0.016                         -0.0800
       0.5   1.00    1.385e-10       0.000            -1.087       -0.1123   [d(slope)/dlogU = +0.0032]
       0.8   0.80    2.609e-10       0.275                         -0.0692
       0.8   1.25    1.381e-10      -0.001                         -0.0708
       0.8   1.00    1.385e-10       0.000            -1.426       -0.1123   [d(slope)/dlogU = -0.0086]
  [PASS] B2 THE LEVER IS THE LARGEST IN THE WHOLE HUNT, and it was never computed before.  The closed-form inversion is a steep function of f_DM, so a 0.1 dex error in the stellar mass-to-light ratio moves the ABSOLUTE a_0 by two to three times that.  Item 16's median a_0 must not be quoted as an M/L-free measurement   (d log a_0/d log Upsilon = -1.087 (f_star = 0.5) to -1.426 (f_star = 0.8); 0.1 dex in Upsilon is 0.11 dex in a_0, against the 0.09 dex that separates the two footings)
  [PASS] B3 AND THE GOOD NEWS, WHICH IS THE POINT OF DOING THIS: the TREND is far more robust than the zero point.  A redshift-independent error in Upsilon shifts every galaxy's a_0 by the same factor and cancels out of the slope almost exactly.  Item 16's d log a_0/dz survives; its absolute a_0 does not   (d(d log a_0/dz)/d log Upsilon = -0.0086 to +0.0032, so a 0.1 dex Upsilon error moves the trend by at most 0.0003 against its own error bar of 0.0642 (0% of it))
  [PASS] B4 the leverage is asymmetric and that is worth recording: raising Upsilon lowers f_DM, and the inversion blows up as f_DM -> 0 because ln(1/f_DM) -> 0.  Galaxies already near baryon domination are the ones that move, so the estimator's tail is the systematic   (at Upsilon x1.25, f_star = 0.8, -1.43 lever and 31 galaxies fall out of the invertible range entirely)

